resolve is a third consumer of the residue format git-span/conflict-residue-boundary declares: it derives its input-shape claim by reading format_residue_markers directly — at most one conflict block on each side of the blank-line separator, anchors before it and why after — so coalescing those blocks again makes resolve refuse the driver's own output. Anchors and why come unconditionally from the live worktree text via --fix's marker-split and clean-source precondition, while unmerged index stages supplement only [config], base for three-way arbitration, and theirs' why — the writer emits ours_why on its non-diverging branch, so theirs is the only side whose why it can leave out of the text and ours' empty why is always the operator's own. resolve canonicalizes marker length before that split because split_conflict_markers recognizes a separator of exactly seven '=', and it recomputes --fix's orphan key-set difference in its own copy rather than sharing a helper, so the two must stay in step on what counts as an orphan; it never calls --fix's partial-write or rename-recovery paths.
